```

Line 299 is the worker of a server instance that shares the bind address of another one. The first instance fails the same way at line 231.

**Cause.** `updateSSLOptions` publishes the update's future before it has completed:

```java
Future<SslContextUpdate> update = sslHelper.updateSslContext(new SSLOptions(options), force, ctx);
sslChannelProvider = update;
```

and the accept path takes its result without checking completion:

```java
worker = ch -> childHandler.accept(ch, sslChannelProvider.result().sslChannelProvider());
// servers sharing the address:
worker = ch -> childHandler.accept(ch, actualServer.sslChannelProvider.result().sslChannelProvider());
```

`SSLHelper#updateSslContext` builds the new `SslChannelProvider` asynchronously, so until it completes `result()` is `null`. `TCPServerBase#sslContextProvider()` reads the same field and returns `null` during an update too. A failed update is not the problem: `updateSslContext` then completes with the previous provider and the error.

### Do you have a reproducer?

Yes, with plain Vert.x. It starts an HTTPS server, opens 4,000 connections from 8 client threads (one request each), and meanwhile calls `updateSSLOptions` with the same key store (`force` = `true`, as for a reloaded certificate):

```java
import io.vertx.core.Vertx;
import io.vertx.core.http.HttpServer;
import io.vertx.core.http.HttpServerOptions;
import io.vertx.core.net.PfxOptions;
import io.vertx.core.net.SSLOptions;

import javax.net.ssl.SSLContext;
import javax.net.ssl.SSLSocket;
import javax.net.ssl.TrustManager;
import javax.net.ssl.X509TrustManager;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.security.cert.X509Certificate;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicBoolean;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

/**
 * New TLS connections fail while HttpServer.updateSSLOptions is in progress.
 *
 * java -cp <vertx-core and netty jars> TlsUpdateRace.java <keystore.p12> <password> <update: true|false> [<pause between updates, ms>]
 */
public class TlsUpdateRace {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        String keystore = args[0];
        String password = args[1];
        boolean update = Boolean.parseBoolean(args[2]);
        long pause = args.length > 3 ? Long.parseLong(args[3]) : 0;

        Vertx vertx = Vertx.vertx();
        PfxOptions pfx = new PfxOptions().setPath(keystore).setPassword(password);
        HttpServer server = vertx.createHttpServer(new HttpServerOptions().setSsl(true).setKeyCertOptions(pfx).setPort(0))
                .requestHandler(request -> request.response().end("ok"));
        server.listen().toCompletionStage().toCompletableFuture().get();
        int port = server.actualPort();

        // Keeps replacing the server's SSL options, as a certificate reload does
        AtomicBoolean running = new AtomicBoolean(true);
        AtomicInteger updates = new AtomicInteger();
        Thread updater = new Thread(() -> {
            while (update && running.get()) {
                try {
                    server.updateSSLOptions(new SSLOptions().setKeyCertOptions(pfx), true).toCompletionStage().toCompletableFuture().get();
                    updates.incrementAndGet();
                    Thread.sleep(pause);
                } catch (Exception e) {
                    e.printStackTrace();
                }
            }
        });
        updater.start();

        // Clients open new connections, each with one request, at the same time
        SSLContext tls = SSLContext.getInstance("TLS");
        tls.init(null, new TrustManager[] { new X509TrustManager() {
            public void checkClientTrusted(X509Certificate[] chain, String authType) {
            }

            public void checkServerTrusted(X509Certificate[] chain, String authType) {
            }

            public X509Certificate[] getAcceptedIssuers() {
                return new X509Certificate[0];
            }
        } }, null);
        AtomicInteger connections = new AtomicInteger();
        AtomicInteger failures = new AtomicInteger();
        List<Thread> clients = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < 8; i++) {
            Thread client = new Thread(() -> {
                for (int j = 0; j < 500; j++) {
                    connections.incrementAndGet();
                    try (SSLSocket socket = (SSLSocket) tls.getSocketFactory().createSocket("localhost", port)) {
                        socket.startHandshake();
                        socket.getOutputStream().write("GET / HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: localhost\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n".getBytes(StandardCharsets.US_ASCII));
                        if (socket.getInputStream().read() < 0) {
                            throw new IOException("closed without an answer");
                        }
                    } catch (IOException e) {
                        failures.incrementAndGet();
                    }
                }
            });
            client.start();
            clients.add(client);
        }
        for (Thread client : clients) {
            client.join();
        }
        running.set(false);
        updater.join();
        System.out.printf("%d connections, %d failed, during %d SSL option updates%n", connections.get(), failures.get(), updates.get());
        vertx.close().toCompletionStage().toCompletableFuture().get();
    }
}
```

### Steps to reproduce

1. Create a key store: `keytool -genkeypair -keystore server.p12 -storetype PKCS12 -storepass password -alias server -dname CN=localhost -keyalg RSA -keysize 2048`
2. Run the reproducer with vertx-core 4.5.35 and its Netty jars on the class path (Java 11 or later runs the source file directly): `java -cp "lib/*" TlsUpdateRace.java server.p12 password <update> [pause]`

Results:

| Run | Connections | Failed | SSL option updates |
|---|---|---|---|
| No updates (`false`) | 4,000 | 0 | 0 |
| Updates back to back (`true`) | 4,000 | 3,957 | 103 |
| One update every 200 ms (`true 200`) | 4,000 | 625 | 17 |
| One update every second (`true 1000`) | 4,000 | 63 | 4 |

Every failure comes with the `NullPointerException` above (here at `TCPServerBase.java:231`, as the reproducer has a single server instance). So each update drops the connections accepted during the tens of milliseconds it takes to build the new SSL context.

### Expected

Connections accepted during an update use the previous SSL configuration (or the new one, once ready); none fail.

### Suggested fix

Publish only completed updates: keep the last completed `SslContextUpdate` in its own volatile field, read by the accept path and by `sslContextProvider()`, and replace it when an update's future completes, as `SslContextProviderReference` does in Vert.x 5. `SSLHelper#updateSslContext` already chains updates in call order, and completes a failed one with the previous provider, so replacing the field on completion keeps the order and never leaves the server without a provider.

### Extra

With certificates or CRLs reloaded every few minutes, every reload drops a burst of new connections. Clients that retry recover; others report errors. A workaround is to call `updateSSLOptions` only when the TLS material has really changed, which shrinks the problem to one burst per real change.
