## Elasticsearch Version

Reproduced on `main`. Affects every version supporting `element_type: bfloat16`, i.e. 9.3+.

## Installed Plugins

None

## Java Version

_bundled_

## OS Version

N/A

## Problem Description

When `dims` is omitted from a `dense_vector` mapping it is inferred from the first indexed value. The
[docs](https://www.elastic.co/docs/reference/elasticsearch/mapping-reference/dense-vector) state:

> `dims` — (Optional, integer) Number of vector dimensions. [...] If `dims` is not specified, it will be
> set to the length of the first vector added to the field.

For `element_type: bfloat16` that inference is wrong when the first value is a base64-encoded string.
`BFloat16Element` extends `FloatElement` and overrides `writeValues`, `readValue`, `getNumBytes` and
`isUnitVector`, but **not** `parseDimensionCount`. It therefore inherits `FloatElement`'s implementation,
which assumes 4 bytes per component:

```java
// FloatElement.parseDimensionCount — DenseVectorFieldMapper.java
case VALUE_STRING -> {
    byte[] decodedVectorBytes = Base64.getDecoder().decode(context.parser().text());
    if (decodedVectorBytes.length % Float.BYTES != 0) {
        throw new ParsingException(..., "Base64 decoded vector byte length [...] is not a multiple of [4]");
    }
    yield decodedVectorBytes.length / Float.BYTES;
}
```

A bfloat16 payload carries `BFloat16.BYTES` (2) per component, so this yields **half** the true dimension
count. There are two user-visible symptoms:

1. **Silent data corruption** when the byte length is a multiple of 4. The field is mapped with half the
   dimensions, and the document is then re-parsed against that mapping. `FloatElement.parseBase64EncodedVector`
   accepts a payload of `dims * Float.BYTES`, which the halved `dims` now satisfies, so the bytes are
   reinterpreted as float32 and indexed without any error. The stored vector has both the wrong cardinality
   and wrong values.
2. **Rejected document** when the byte length is not a multiple of 4 (any odd dimension count). Indexing
   fails with an error citing `[4]` as the required multiple, which is misleading for a field whose element
   type uses 2 bytes.

Note the indexing path itself is correct — `FloatElement.parseBase64EncodedVector`
(`DenseVectorFieldMapper.java:1477-1480`) explicitly accepts *either* width:

```java
if (byteBuffer.remaining() == dims * Float.BYTES) {
    byteBuffer.asFloatBuffer().get(decodedVector);
} else if (byteBuffer.remaining() == dims * BFloat16.BYTES) {
    BFloat16.bFloat16ToFloat(byteBuffer, decodedVector);
}
```

Only dimension *inference* is missing the bfloat16 case. Specifying `dims` explicitly works correctly and
is a viable workaround.

## Steps to Reproduce

Each case maps a `dense_vector` with `element_type: bfloat16` and **no** `dims`, then indexes one
base64-encoded bfloat16 payload.

### Case 1 — `dims` inferred as 2 instead of 4; document accepted with corrupted values

```json
PUT /bfloat16-dims
{
  "mappings": {
    "properties": {
      "my_vector": {
        "type": "dense_vector",
        "element_type": "bfloat16",
        "index": false
      }
    }
  }
}

PUT /bfloat16-dims/_doc/1?refresh=true
{
  "my_vector": "P8BAAMBQQIA="
}

GET /bfloat16-dims/_mapping/field/my_vector

GET /bfloat16-dims/_search
{
  "docvalue_fields": [ "my_vector" ]
}
```

`P8BAAMBQQIA=` is the 4-dimensional vector `[1.5, 2.0, -3.25, 4.0]` as big-endian bfloat16 —
`3f c0 40 00 c0 50 40 80`, 8 bytes.

| Request | Expected | Actual |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `GET /bfloat16-dims/_mapping/field/my_vector` | `"dims": 4` | `"dims": 2` |
| `PUT /bfloat16-dims/_doc/1` | `201 Created` | `201 Created` (no error surfaced) |
| `GET /bfloat16-dims/_search` docvalues | `[1.5, 2.0, -3.25, 4.0]` | `[1.501953125, -3.2539367676]` |

The 8-byte payload is divided by 4 rather than 2, giving `dims: 2`. The document is then re-parsed against
that mapping, where 8 bytes satisfies `dims * Float.BYTES`, so `3f c0 40 00` is read as a single float32
instead of two bfloat16 components — and likewise `c0 50 40 80`.

### Case 2 — document rejected outright for an odd dimension count

```json
PUT /bfloat16-dims-odd
{
  "mappings": {
    "properties": {
      "my_vector": {
        "type": "dense_vector",
        "element_type": "bfloat16",
        "index": false
      }
    }
  }
}

PUT /bfloat16-dims-odd/_doc/1
{
  "my_vector": "P8BAAMBQ"
}
```

`P8BAAMBQ` is the 3-dimensional vector `[1.5, 2.0, -3.25]` as big-endian bfloat16 — `3f c0 40 00 c0 50`,
6 bytes.

| Request | Expected | Actual |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `PUT /bfloat16-dims-odd/_doc/1` | `201 Created`, field mapped with `"dims": 3` | `400 Bad Request` |

```json
{
  "error": {
    "type": "document_parsing_exception",
    "reason": "[1:16] failed to parse: Failed to parse object: Base64 decoded vector byte length [6] is not a multiple of [4]"
  },
  "status": 400
}
```

### Control — the same payload works when `dims` is explicit

```json
PUT /bfloat16-dims-explicit
{
  "mappings": {
    "properties": {
      "my_vector": {
        "type": "dense_vector",
        "element_type": "bfloat16",
        "dims": 4,
        "index": false
      }
    }
  }
}

PUT /bfloat16-dims-explicit/_doc/1?refresh=true
{
  "my_vector": "P8BAAMBQQIA="
}

GET /bfloat16-dims-explicit/_search
{
  "docvalue_fields": [ "my_vector" ]
}
```

Returns `[1.5, 2.0, -3.25, 4.0]`, confirming the indexing path handles the 2-byte width correctly and that
only dimension inference is broken. Setting `dims` explicitly is therefore a workaround.

## Logs (if relevant)

N/A

