Restoring a cluster snapshot that still contains searchable-snapshot indices can leave those indices **red** when ILM (or anything else) has already deleted their backing snapshot. `ignore_unavailable` on the [restore snapshot API](https://www.elastic.co/docs/api/doc/elasticsearch/operation/operation-snapshot-restore) does not help: it only skips index names that are not in the snapshot. These indices **are** in the snapshot; restore recreates them, shard recovery hits `SnapshotMissingException` (e.g., missing `.dat` file), and the cluster stays red.

This shows up in particular on ECH create-from-snapshot: the constructor waits for green, the plan hangs on `wait-until-snapshot-restored` (`Indices still initializing: []`), and the deployment stays **Being Created** (and the user may not delete the red indices if REST is inaccessible). Self-managed restores have the same red leftovers, without the orchestration hang.

### Why a control plane workaround is not enough

Control plane is looking at tolerating permanently red indices while waiting for restore. That can unblock ECH access, but Elasticsearch does not expose a robust "this shard will never recover" signal, so the orchestrator has to guess (quiet-red window + allocation explain + a red-index fraction cap). A large frozen tier can still fail that cap, and the cluster is still left red.

We should handle this in restore itself so those indices are not created (or are omitted) and the restored cluster can come up without those red shards.

### Proposed direction

A restore option / whole-cluster restore mode that omits searchable-snapshot indices whose backing snapshot is gone, analogous to `ignore_unavailable` but for missing backing snapshots, not missing index names. That is one of the things a proper ["restore this whole cluster from snapshot" mode](https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/issues/98212) would need; it is also useful on its own for clone/DR of clusters that use ILM searchable snapshots.

Related docs: [Back up and restore searchable snapshots](https://www.elastic.co/docs/deploy-manage/tools/snapshot-and-restore/searchable-snapshots#back-up-restore-searchable-snapshots).

