body_text:
### Elasticsearch Version

9.4.3

### Installed Plugins

_No response_

### Java Version

openjdk 21.0.12.1 2026-08-18

### OS Version

Darwin Kernel Version 25.6.0

### Problem Description

Before 9.4.0, latest transforms were single pass and only read the documents in its sync window.

Since Elasticsearch 9.4 transforms have included https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/pull/142856 which adjusts the way transforms are calculated. Transforms now perform a second pass to search historical data to ensure that late events where the sort field and sync field disagree are able to be properly handled. The implementation of the second pass is currently unoptimised when multiple fields are provided as it passes separate lists of values to compare. The implementation can be optimised by defaulting to passing the exact tuples that were observed.

### Steps to Reproduce

Below logic sets up a sample index with transform and then checks the documents that are modified.
Note that the transform utilises a `unique_key` of `["order_id", "region"]` and the latter update only contains two documents. The expectation then is that there should only be two updates made in the target index (and only two pairs searched for historically). However, the documents indexed increments by `4` instead of `2`.
```
PUT _ingest/pipeline/stamp
{ "processors": [ { "set": { "field": "event_ingested", "value": "{{{_ingest.timestamp}}}" } } ] }

PUT orders
{
  "settings": {
    "index.default_pipeline": "stamp",
    "index.search.slowlog.threshold.query.trace": "0ms"
  },
  "mappings": { "properties": {
    "order_id": { "type": "keyword" }, "region": { "type": "keyword" }, "status": { "type": "keyword" },
    "updated_at": { "type": "date" }, "event_ingested": { "type": "date" }
  } }
}


