### Elasticsearch Version

main

### Installed Plugins

_No response_

### Java Version

_bundled_

### OS Version

Darwin Kernel Version 25.6.0: Tue Aug 18 17:48:46 PDT 2026; root:xnu-12377.161.15.700.19~2/RELEASE_ARM64_T6041 arm64

### Problem Description

## Description

`allow_write_after_shrink` exists so ingest can continue on the shrunken index. It defaults to `false`. When it is `true`, a write through an alias (or the data stream) during the shrink is still a non-retryable `403`, and that document is not on the shrunken index. A later write through the same alias succeeds.

We do not want clients to retry `403`s. Logstash is being asked to dead-letter document-level `403`s ([logstash-output-elasticsearch#1284](https://github.com/logstash-plugins/logstash-output-elasticsearch/issues/1284)). This option needs to stop surfacing that `403` for writes it intends to accept.

## What happens

Shrink's `readonly` step sets `index.blocks.write` on the source (`FORBIDDEN/8/index write (api)`, HTTP 403, `retryable=false`). Resize copies that setting onto the shrunken index. ILM then points the aliases, or the data stream backing index, at the shrunken index, deletes the source, and only afterwards clears `index.blocks.write` when `allow_write_after_shrink` is `true`.

A write in that window fails with: `cluster_block_exception index [name] blocked by: [FORBIDDEN/8/index write (api)]`

The rejected document is not replayed. With the default `allow_write_after_shrink: false`, the block stays and a later write also fails, which is consistent. Frozen and mounted searchable snapshots return this same block and stay blocked.

The `403` cannot tell the client that the block will be cleared. Shrink can still be slow or fail after the block is set.

## Expected

With `allow_write_after_shrink: true`, a client that keeps writing through the alias or data stream should not receive a non-retryable `403` for a document the shrunken index is meant to accept.

Ideas:

* Use a retryable status code (e.g., 503 or 429) code during this window
* ES internally queues the writes and forwards them to the shrunken index later. but may also need some limit.
* Use a new type of write block (if that's needed, Team:Distributed may help with the new write block)

### Steps to Reproduce

## Steps to reproduce

1. Create an index with 2 primary shards, 0 replicas, and a write alias. Index one document through the alias.
2. Apply an ILM policy whose warm phase is `shrink` with `number_of_shards: 1` and `allow_write_after_shrink: true`.
3. After `index.blocks.write` is set, and before the shrunken index has that setting cleared, index a second document through the alias.
4. That request returns `403` / `cluster_block_exception` / `FORBIDDEN/8/index write (api)`. The document is not indexed.
5. After the warm phase completes, `index.blocks.write` is unset on the shrunken index. A third document indexed through the alias succeeds. The id from step 3 is `404`.

The window is short on a small index. Holding the `readonly` step open makes it easy to observe. That pause must not ship.

Here's a diff for a test that reproduces the above:

```
diff --git a/x-pack/plugin/core/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/core/ilm/ReadOnlyStep.java b/x-pack/plugin/core/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/core/ilm/ReadOnlyStep.java
index 916472ee53c4..b1ff1b240838 100644
--- a/x-pack/plugin/core/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/core/ilm/ReadOnlyStep.java
+++ b/x-pack/plugin/core/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/core/ilm/ReadOnlyStep.java
@@ -51,6 +51,13 @@ public class ReadOnlyStep extends AsyncActionStep {
                     if (response.isAcknowledged() == false) {
                         throw new ElasticsearchException("read only add block index request failed to be acknowledged");
                     }
+                    // TEMPORARY. Do not release.
+                    try {
+                        Thread.sleep(10_000L);
+                    } catch (InterruptedException e) {
+                        Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
+                        throw new ElasticsearchException("interrupted while paused after setting the write block", e);
+                    }
                     l.onResponse(null);
                 })
             );
diff --git a/x-pack/plugin/ilm/src/javaRestTest/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/ilm/actions/ShrinkActionIT.java b/x-pack/plugin/ilm/src/javaRestTest/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/ilm/actions/ShrinkActionIT.java
index 5b7a6e300e42..9c9a20752e12 100644
--- a/x-pack/plugin/ilm/src/javaRestTest/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/ilm/actions/ShrinkActionIT.java
+++ b/x-pack/plugin/ilm/src/javaRestTest/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/ilm/actions/ShrinkActionIT.java
@@ -52,6 +52,7 @@ import static org.elasticsearch.xpack.TimeSeriesRestDriver.indexDocument;
 import static org.elasticsearch.xpack.TimeSeriesRestDriver.updatePolicy;
 import static org.elasticsearch.xpack.TimeSeriesRestDriver.waitAndGetShrinkIndexName;
 import static org.elasticsearch.xpack.TimeSeriesRestDriver.waitAndGetShrinkIndexNameWithExtraClusterStateChange;
+import static org.hamcrest.Matchers.containsString;
 import static org.hamcrest.Matchers.equalTo;
 import static org.hamcrest.Matchers.notNullValue;
 import static org.hamcrest.Matchers.nullValue;
@@ -467,4 +468,68 @@ public class ShrinkActionIT extends IlmESRestTestCase {
         // check that actually wrote to index
         assertBusy(() -> assertDocCount(client(), index, 1));
     }
