**Elasticsearch Version:** 9.5.2 through 9.5.5 and `main`, as the coordinating node. The trigger is a fetch from a data node that does a classic fetch: any 9.4.0–9.5.1 node with default settings, or a node with `search.fetch_phase_chunked_enabled: false`.

**Installed Plugins:** none

**Java Version:** _bundled_

**OS Version:** any

### Problem Description

When a coordinating node asks a data node for a chunked fetch and the data node does a classic fetch instead, the coordinator decodes the response in the wrong format. No error is raised. What users see depends on the sort:

| Search | Hits from the affected shard |
|---|---|
| Sorted by relevance (the default) | Order and `_score` are correct, because the coordinator re-applies query-phase scores. Each hit gains a stray `"_rank": 33554431`. |
| Rank retrievers such as RRF | Correct; rank and score are re-applied. |
| Sorted by a field | `_score` is `-2.0000608` instead of `null`, plus the stray `_rank`. |
| Sorted by a field with `track_scores: true` | Garbage `_score` and the stray `_rank`. When the scores don't rise with the sort order, the hits are also reordered: documents come back in the wrong positions, each carrying another document's sort value. |

With default settings this happens on every multi-shard fetch from a 9.4.x, 9.5.0 or 9.5.1 node by a 9.5.2+ coordinator, so rolling upgrades to 9.5.2+ are affected.

How it happens:

1. **The coordinator asks for a chunked fetch** whenever its own `search.fetch_phase_chunked_enabled` is on and the data node's transport version supports `chunked_fetch_doc_id_order`. CCS, scroll and, in 9.5.x, reindex are excluded ([`SearchTransportService.java:405`](https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/blob/71139d9bac940a38502fe15bda0755b75ce6cee0/server/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/action/search/SearchTransportService.java#L405); [v9.5.2 `:383`](https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/blob/v9.5.2/server/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/action/search/SearchTransportService.java#L383)). It can't see the data node's setting.
2. **The data node decides for itself.** It uses chunked fetch only if its own setting is on and it already has an open connection to the coordinator ([`:789`](https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/blob/71139d9bac940a38502fe15bda0755b75ce6cee0/server/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/action/search/SearchTransportService.java#L789)). Otherwise it does a classic fetch, and because the request named a coordinator, it also fills `lastChunkBytes` through `populateBwcLastChunkBytes` ([`:841-847`](https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/blob/71139d9bac940a38502fe15bda0755b75ce6cee0/server/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/action/search/SearchTransportService.java#L841-L847)).
3. **That method writes the pre-doc-ID-order format,** each hit with no position prefix ([`:900-912`](https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/blob/71139d9bac940a38502fe15bda0755b75ce6cee0/server/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/action/search/SearchTransportService.java#L900-L912)). Its comments say the format is for old coordinators. The condition `hasCoordinator && chunkWriter == null` doesn't check the coordinator's version, though.
4. **The coordinator reads a position before every hit** ([`TransportFetchPhaseCoordinationAction.java:242-244`](https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/blob/71139d9bac940a38502fe15bda0755b75ce6cee0/server/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/search/fetch/chunk/TransportFetchPhaseCoordinationAction.java#L242-L244)) and builds the shard result from those decoded hits only. It never looks at the correct classic hits the data node also sent.
   - The position comes from the first byte of the hit's score. A fetched hit's score is `NaN` (`7f c0 00 00`) unless the search sorts by a field with `track_scores`, so the position is usually 127.
   - The hit is then read starting one byte late. The score is taken from the remaining score bytes plus the first byte of the `rank` vInt, and the rank from the rest of that vInt, giving `-2.0000608` and `33554431`. The stream realigns at the id, so ids and `_source` come out right.
   - `SearchPhaseController#getHits` ([`:337-361`](https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/blob/71139d9bac940a38502fe15bda0755b75ce6cee0/server/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/action/search/SearchPhaseController.java#L337-L361)) takes the shard's hits in that order. It overwrites the score for relevance sorts and the rank for rank retrievers, which gives the table above.

**Why upgrades hit it with default settings.** In 9.4.0–9.4.8 and 9.5.0–9.5.1, `search.fetch_phase_chunked_enabled` defaults to the `chunked_fetch_phase_enabled` feature flag ([v9.5.1 `SearchService.java:276-277`](https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/blob/v9.5.1/server/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/search/SearchService.java#L276-L277)). Feature flags are off in release builds ([`FeatureFlag.java:72-76`](https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/blob/71139d9bac940a38502fe15bda0755b75ce6cee0/server/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/common/util/FeatureFlag.java#L72-L76)). Those releases still support `chunked_fetch_doc_id_order` and have the same fallback ([v9.5.1 `:799`](https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/blob/v9.5.1/server/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/action/search/SearchTransportService.java#L799), [v9.4.8 `:767`](https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/blob/v9.4.8/server/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/action/search/SearchTransportService.java#L767)). From 9.5.2 (#156086) the setting defaults to `true`, so an upgraded coordinator requests chunked fetches from nodes that always fall back. Single-shard searches and fetches from the local node aren't affected.

**Why CI doesn't show it.** The BWC versions in `.ci/bwcVersions` for 9.4 and 9.5 are 9.4.9 and 9.5.6. Both are unreleased snapshot builds, where chunked fetch is on (feature flags are on in snapshots, and 9.5.6 defaults to `true`), so their data nodes never fall back. No released 9.4.0–9.5.1 build is tested, and nothing tests `populateBwcLastChunkBytes`.

**Expected:** a fetch that falls back returns the same hits, scores, sort values and order as one that doesn't, with no `_rank` outside rank retrievers.

### Steps to Reproduce

This integration test runs three nodes in one JVM with real transport: a master, a data node with `search.fetch_phase_chunked_enabled: false` that holds both shards of the index, and a coordinating-only node with chunked fetch on. The test below fails on `main`. Two sibling tests also fail: one searches with `match` on `text` and asserts that every hit has `rank` -1, and one sorts by `value` without `track_scores` and asserts that every `_score` is `NaN`.

```java
@ESIntegTestCase.ClusterScope(scope = ESIntegTestCase.Scope.TEST, numDataNodes = 0, numClientNodes = 0)
public class ChunkedFetchClassicFallbackIT extends ESIntegTestCase {
    private static final String INDEX = "classic_fallback";
    private static final int DOCS = 12;

    private String startClusterAndIndex() throws Exception {
        internalCluster().startMasterOnlyNode();
        String classicFetchNode = internalCluster().startDataOnlyNode(
            Settings.builder().put(SearchService.FETCH_PHASE_CHUNKED_ENABLED.getKey(), false).build());
        String coordinator = internalCluster().startCoordinatingOnlyNode(
            Settings.builder().put(SearchService.FETCH_PHASE_CHUNKED_ENABLED.getKey(), true).build());
        assertAcked(prepareCreate(INDEX).setSettings(Settings.builder()
                .put(IndexMetadata.SETTING_NUMBER_OF_SHARDS, 2)
                .put(IndexMetadata.SETTING_NUMBER_OF_REPLICAS, 0)
                .put("index.routing.allocation.require._name", classicFetchNode))
            .setMapping("value", "type=integer", "text", "type=text"));
        List<IndexRequestBuilder> docs = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 1; i <= DOCS; i++) {
            docs.add(prepareIndex(INDEX).setId(Integer.toString(i)).setSource("value", i, "text", "fox " + "quick ".repeat(i)));
        }
        indexRandom(true, docs);
        ensureGreen(INDEX);
        return coordinator;
    }

    public void testFieldSortWithTrackedScores() throws Exception {
        String coordinator = startClusterAndIndex();
        assertNoFailuresAndResponse(
            internalCluster().client(coordinator).prepareSearch(INDEX)
                .setQuery(QueryBuilders.functionScoreQuery(ScoreFunctionBuilders.fieldValueFactorFunction("value"))
                    .boostMode(CombineFunction.REPLACE))
                .addSort("value", SortOrder.DESC)
                .setTrackScores(true)
                .setSize(DOCS),
            response -> {
                SearchHit[] hits = response.getHits().getHits();
                for (int i = 0; i < hits.length; i++) {
                    int value = ((Number) hits[i].getSourceAsMap().get("value")).intValue();
                    assertThat(value, equalTo(DOCS - i));
                    assertThat(((Number) hits[i].getSortValues()[0]).intValue(), equalTo(value));
                    assertThat(hits[i].getScore(), equalTo((float) value));
                    assertThat(hits[i].getRank(), equalTo(-1));
                }
            });
    }
}
```

Results on `main` (71139d9bac9):

| Test | Expected | Returned |
|---|---|---|
| Relevance search | `rank` -1 on every hit | `rank` 33554431 on every hit |
| Sort by `value` | `_score` NaN | `_score` -2.0000608 |
| Sort by `value` desc, `track_scores` | doc 12, 11, 10, …, 1, each with its own value as sort value and score | doc 7, 1, 6, 3, 12, 9, 5, 4, 2, 8, 11, 10 with sort values 12, 11, 10, …, 1, so the first hit is doc 7 reported with sort value 12, and scores like -3.689461E19 and 3.57E-43 |

At the byte level the coordinator takes the first byte of each hit's score as its position (`7f` for a `NaN` score), reads the hit one byte late, and realigns at the id. That's why ids and `_source` look plausible while score, rank and order don't.

A 9.5.1 node in a 9.5.2+ cluster behaves like the data node here, since its setting defaults to off. I ran the in-process test above, not a real mixed-version upgrade.

### Proposed fix

- **Coordinator, which also covers the released 9.4.x–9.5.1 nodes:** a real chunked fetch always returns `SearchHits.empty(...)` from the data node ([`FetchPhase.java:618`](https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/blob/71139d9bac940a38502fe15bda0755b75ce6cee0/server/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/search/fetch/FetchPhase.java#L618); the same in the v9.4.0, v9.4.8, v9.5.0, v9.5.2 and v9.5.5 tags), while the fallback carries the classic hits. When `dataNodeResult.hits()` isn't empty, return `dataNodeResult` itself (with an `incRef`) instead of decoding `lastChunkBytes`. That also keeps its profile and directory metrics.
- **Data node:** in the fallback, write a `vInt` position before each hit when the coordinator supports `chunked_fetch_doc_id_order`. Every released coordinator that names itself in the request does, so this also fixes responses to coordinators that haven't been fixed yet. Leaving `lastChunkBytes` out instead would make an unfixed coordinator drop those hits as partial results.
- **Hardening (optional):** reject positions outside `[0, expectedTotalDocs)` and duplicate positions in `FetchPhaseResponseStream`.
- **Tests:** the integration test above; a coordinator unit test in `TransportFetchPhaseCoordinationActionTests` that answers with the fallback format; a test of the fallback writer; and, ideally, a rolling-upgrade test whose old nodes set `search.fetch_phase_chunked_enabled: false`, since the BWC snapshots have it on.
- **Backport** to 9.5.

I'm preparing a fix with these tests and will open a PR that references this issue.

