body_text:
### ESLint version

v10.12.0

### What problem do you want to solve?

`ESLintCircularFixesWarning` tells you *that* a file hit circular fixes, but not *which rules* are in conflict:

```
(node:12) ESLintCircularFixesWarning: Circular fixes detected while fixing /path/to/file.css.ts. It is likely that you have conflicting rules in your configuration.
```

In a config with many plugins (typescript-eslint, perfectionist, prettier, framework plugins, ...) the only way to find the pair is to bisect the config by hand: run with `--fix-dry-run -f json` and turn rules off one at a time until the warning disappears. With many files the output is also just one identical line per file, so it is hard to tell whether they all share the same conflict.

[#17609](https://github.com/eslint/eslint/issues/17609) originally asked for exactly this ("say which plugins are possibly conflicting"), but the change that closed it only added the filename to the message.

The information needed already exists inside `verifyAndFix`: every pass has `messages` carrying `ruleId` and `fix`, and `SourceCodeFixer.applyFixes` knows which problems it applied. The warning is emitted by `emitCircularFixesWarning(filename)`, which receives nothing but the file name.

### Reproduction

https://stackblitz.com/edit/qnuqxypp (run `npm start`; the terminal shows the warning with only the file name, no rule names).

It uses two rules whose fixes undo each other, no plugins needed:

```js
// eslint.config.js
const swap = (from, to) => ({
  meta: { type: "suggestion", fixable: "code", schema: [], messages: { m: `use ${to}` } },
  create: (context) => ({
    Identifier(node) {
      if (node.name === from) {
        context.report({ node, messageId: "m", fix: (fixer) => fixer.replaceText(node, to) });
      }
    },
  }),
});

export default [
  {
    files: ["**/*.js"],
    plugins: {
      demo: { rules: { "prefer-bar": swap("foo", "bar"), "prefer-foo": swap("bar", "foo") } },
    },
    rules: { "demo/prefer-bar": "error", "demo/prefer-foo": "error" },
  },
];
```

```js
// demo.js
const foo = 1;
```

```
$ eslint --fix-dry-run demo.js
(node:12) ESLintCircularFixesWarning: Circular fixes detected while fixing /path/to/demo.js. It is likely that you have conflicting rules in your configuration.
```

Real-world case where I hit it: [`perfectionist/sort-objects`](https://perfectionist.dev/rules/sort-objects) (alphabetical) vs [`vanilla-extract/concentric-order`](https://github.com/antebudimir/eslint-plugin-vanilla-extract#vanilla-extractconcentric-order) (concentric CSS property order) on every `*.css.ts` file (nine files in one run, same message each time, no rule names).


### What do you think is the correct solution?

Include the `ruleId`s of the fixes that were applied during the two repeating passes in the existing `ESLintCircularFixesWarning`, and name them instead of the generic hint, e.g.:

```
(node:12) ESLintCircularFixesWarning: Circular fixes detected while fixing /path/to/file.css.ts.
