### Describe the bug

In `packages/common/src/url.ts`, the `isLocalLink` function determines whether a link belongs to the local application (navigating within `_self`) or is an external link (opening in `_blank`):

```typescript
export const isLocalLink = (link: string | null) => {
  return !!(link?.includes(location.origin) || link?.startsWith("/"));
};

Using link?.includes(location.origin) and link?.startsWith("/") introduces two issues:

Origin Spoofing / Tab Hijacking: Any external URL that merely contains location.origin as a substring (e.g. https://excalidraw.com.attacker.com or https://attacker.com/?ref=https://excalidraw.com) evaluates to true. This opens the attacker URL in _self, enabling phishing / session hijacking.

Protocol-Relative URLs: A URL starting with // (e.g. //attacker.com) satisfies link.startsWith("/"), but will navigate the user to an external domain in _self.

Expected Behavior
Only URLs whose origin strictly matches location.origin (or true relative paths starting with / but not //) should return true.

External links containing the domain as a substring or protocol-relative links must return false (so they safely open in _blank).

Proposed Solution
Validate strictly using the WHATWG new URL(link, location.origin) API:

typescript
export const isLocalLink = (link: string | null) => {
  if (!link || link.startsWith("//")) {
    return false;
  }
  if (link.startsWith("/")) {
    return true;
  }
  try {
    const url = new URL(link, location.origin);
    return url.origin === location.origin;
  } catch {
    return false;
  }
};

