### Summary

In the `--report-glean` output, a method call on a receiver typed as a `Literal` or `LiteralString` names the callee without its class: `title` instead of `str.title`. The same call on a plain `str` receiver is named `str.title`. The bare name appears in `python.CalleeToCaller.4` and in the `python.xrefs.XRefsByFile.1` target at the method token. This makes a consumer unable to tell `str.title` from any other function named `title`.

The behaviour is the same in 1.2.0 and 1.4.0. In 1.4.0 it reaches more code, because a `StrEnum` member's `.value` is now inferred as a literal. So `self.value.title()` changed from `str.title` in 1.2.0 to `title` in 1.4.0.

### Reproduction

`repro.py`:

```python
from enum import StrEnum
from typing import Literal, LiteralString


def plain(word: str) -> str:
    return word.title()


def literal(word: Literal["red"]) -> str:
    return word.title()


def literal_string(word: LiteralString) -> str:
    return word.title()


def joined(parts: list[str]) -> str:
    return ", ".join(parts)


class Colour(StrEnum):
    RED = "red"

    def label(self) -> str:
        return self.value.title()
```

```
pyrefly check --report-glean out/ repro.py
```

Callee per caller, read from `python.CalleeToCaller.4`:

| Caller | 1.2.0 | 1.4.0 | Expected |
|---|---|---|---|
| `repro.plain` | `str.title` | `str.title` | `str.title` |
| `repro.literal` | `title` | `title` | `str.title` |
| `repro.literal_string` | `title` | `title` | `str.title` |
| `repro.joined` | `join` | `join` | `str.join` |
| `repro.Colour.label` | `str.title` | `title` | `str.title` |

### Expected

A method resolved through a `Literal[...]` or `LiteralString` receiver is named by the class that defines it (`str.title`, `str.join`), the same as for a plain `str` receiver. The literal type promotes to its class for attribute lookup, so the qualified name is available.

### Environment

pyrefly 1.2.0 and 1.4.0 (installed with `uv tool`), macOS, Python 3.13. Default config: no `pyrefly.toml`, no other flags.

